<SECTION image-layout="blank" vertical-align="start">
  <DIV class="body">
    <H3>Market Fundamentals</H3>
    <COLUMNS colWidths="[33,33,34]">
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Market Size</B></H4>
        <P>The global climate tech market reached $1.2T in 2024, representing 18% growth from 2023. Energy storage dominates with $347B in deployed capital, followed by renewable generation at $298B. Carbon management technologies captured $89B, marking a 156% increase as corporate buyers embraced removal credits.</P>
        <P><B>Geographic Distribution</B> North America commands 34% market share, driven by IRA incentives and corporate procurement. Europe holds 29%, accelerated by REPowerEU mandates. China's 28% share reflects manufacturing scale in solar and batteries, while emerging markets represent 9% but show fastest growth rates.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Investment Flow</B></H4>
        <P>Venture capital deployment shifted toward later-stage rounds as early technologies mature. Series B+ rounds captured 67% of $48B total climate VC in 2024, up from 52% in 2022. Hardware-intensive sectors like energy storage and industrial decarbonization attracted 71% of growth capital.</P>
        <P><B>Corporate Strategic Investment</B> reached $23B, with utilities leading at $8.9B deployed. Oil majors contributed $6.2B, primarily in carbon capture and hydrogen. Tech companies allocated $4.1B toward grid software and demand response platforms. Family offices and sovereign wealth funds emerged as significant players, contributing $11B across all stages.</P>
      </DIV>
      <DIV>
        <H4><B>Policy Drivers</B></H4>
        <P>Regulatory frameworks in EU and US created $127B in direct climate tech incentives during 2024. The Inflation Reduction Act generated $89B in tax credit utilization, while EU's Green Deal Industrial Plan mobilized €38B in member state co-investment. China's carbon market expansion to petrochemicals and aviation added 2.1 billion tons of coverage.</P>
        <P><B>Compliance Markets</B> Carbon pricing mechanisms now cover 23% of global emissions, up from 16% in 2021. California's cap-and-trade system reached $28/ton CO2, while EU ETS averaged €85/ton. Corporate voluntary commitments totaled 847 companies with science-based targets, driving $34B in procurement of climate solutions.</P>
      </DIV>
    </COLUMNS>
  </DIV>
</SECTION>